From 39db3a8a04fda86ce899b4db7a0b487c0959f086 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ayiejosh <132535860+ayiejosh@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 00:26:58 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 221/294] gallium/pvr: PBE state words belong in the
 pixel-event program, not the fragment stream

Comparing the CCCB commands the kernel hands the firmware for our draw and for the working Vulkan
driver (traced in round 4, re-traced here) showed the fragment stream parsed into the wrong thing.
The cause is a layout fact the earlier stream port had wrong: the fragment firmware stream carries
PBE *register* words only - PBESTATE_REG_WORD0 (memory layout, encoded stride, channel swizzles) and
REG_WORD1 (clip extents) plus a zero word - while the target address and output format are
PBESTATE_STATE_WORD0/WORD1 and live in the pixel-event PDS program's emit words
(pvr_arch_job_common.c pvr_pbe_state_words, pvr_arch_cmd_buffer.c per-job fragment programs). Writing
the state words into the stream made the kernel parse a command whose PBE block was a stream header
and garbage.

pvr_csb_pbe_words() now builds the register words in the reference's encoding; the probe builds
them before the stream from the target's stride/width/height rather than over its own source buffer.

Also unified the probe onto one VM context: the shader/PDS/USC programs were placed in the screen's
VM context while vm_roundtrip created a second context and submitted jobs there, so the firmware
read its programs through a context in which those heap addresses were not mapped. The draw test now
uses the screen's VM (the same one the programs are placed in), which is what any real driver does.

Verification: probe exits 0 ALL PASS under the noop shim; on the real driver the firmware still does
not complete the kick. The remaining missing piece is visible in the reference now: CR_EVENT_PIXEL_PDS_DATA
points at a per-job pixel-event program built by pco (pvr_usc_eot) with the PBE state words as emit
words and wrapped in a PDS DOUTU program; this driver still passes a hardcoded offset with no such
program behind it.
